        <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl lg:text-4xl text-ink leading-tight mb-5">7 Things to Know Before Starting Therapy</h1>
        <p class="text-ink-muted text-lg leading-relaxed">Taking the first step into therapy requires courage. Clarifying your expectations beforehand both reduces anxiety and helps you get the most from therapy.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-12 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <div class="prose-custom space-y-8">

          <div>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Many people view therapy through a distorted lens: expectations such as "You must be very unwell," "You have to tell everything," or "You'll be fixed in a few sessions" can delay taking that first step. This article is written to clear up that uncertainty.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-5">

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">1</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Psychologist–client fit matters more than anything</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Research shows that the greatest factor determining the effectiveness of therapy is not the psychologist's technical knowledge, but the trust built with the client. If you do not feel safe and understood in the first couple of sessions, looking for a different psychologist is both normal and recommended.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">2</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">The first session is an assessment, not therapy</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">In the first session the psychologist tries to understand you and the reason for your visit. Let go of the pressure to "find a solution straight away." This session is also your opportunity to get to know the psychologist and gauge whether you feel comfortable.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">3</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Change takes time — and doesn't move in a straight line</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Some weeks you will feel progress; other weeks may feel harder. This is normal. The pattern of "two steps forward, one step back" is very common in therapy. Approaching the process with patience is one of the most important factors in determining the outcome.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">4</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">You don't have to share everything</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">You set your own privacy boundaries. Saying "I don't want to talk about this" is completely valid, and a good psychologist should never push you. Trust builds over time; your disclosures naturally deepen alongside it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">5</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Setting goals speeds progress</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Asking yourself "What do I want to gain from this therapy?" before you begin is very useful. Even if you don't have a clear answer, share that with your psychologist. Shared goals make it easier for each session to stay focused.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">6</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Between-session "homework" is part of the process</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Particularly in structured therapies like CBT, your psychologist may suggest exercises or thought journals between sessions. These deepen the session's impact. If you weren't able to do them, that itself is useful material to discuss.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
              <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">7</div>
              <div>
                <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">You can end therapy whenever you choose</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Therapy is not an endless obligation. When you have achieved your goals or simply no longer wish to continue, you can close the process healthily by informing your psychologist. A good psychologist will not try to keep you — they will respect your decision.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>

          <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-primary/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">Therapy is not something only "weak" people do</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">On the contrary, going to therapy is a decision to invest in your own mental health. Millions of athletes, executives, artists and ordinary people around the world use therapy as a tool for growth. Going when there is no acute problem is also extremely valuable.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-accent/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-accent/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">How frequent should sessions be?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">One session per week is the most common starting point. Fortnightly sessions can also be effective given time or budget constraints. You and your psychologist can decide together on the frequency that best suits your needs.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
